# The `tabula::unreachable-heavy` fixture: a matrix a quarter made of claims
# that something cannot happen.
#
# Three UNREACHABLE cells of twelve is 25%, which is `unreachableHeavyPercent`
# exactly. The fixture sits on the boundary on purpose, as `payload-hoist` does
# for its threshold: an implementation comparing with `>` rather than `>=`
# passes every other fixture and fails this one.
#
# Each claim is individually reasonable -- the dialer UI disables `Dial` once
# a call is up, and an `Ack` cannot arrive before anything was dialled. That is
# the lint's point. One or two such assertions are what the cell kind is for
# (`toggle` has one and must stay silent); a quarter of the matrix says the
# action alphabet is describing a different machine from the one declared.
#
# The traces never visit an UNREACHABLE cell. Doing so traps, in every
# language, by design (`spec/cells.md`), and the trace format has no outcome
# for "this aborts the process".
#
# Quiet elsewhere: every row has a GO, so no `no-static-exit`; no row or
# column is all IGNORE (`Ping` lives on Up's EMIT); 4 of 12 IGNORE is 33%;
# a HANDLE and the UNREACHABLE cells make the matrix not fully static, so
# `no-static-entry` stays gated even though `Up` is reached only by the
# HANDLE; no payloads.

machine Link
initial Down
states  Down Dialing Up
actions Dial Ack Hangup Ping

Down    | GO(Dialing) | UNREACHABLE | IGNORE   | IGNORE
Dialing | UNREACHABLE | HANDLE      | GO(Down) | IGNORE
Up      | UNREACHABLE | IGNORE      | GO(Down) | EMIT(Pong)
